    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/george-v"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-v.jpg" alt="George V" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_George_1923_LCCN2014715558_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bassano Ltd, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The present United Kingdom took shape with the partition of Ireland. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland</a>, created by the Acts of Union of 1800, had faced a war of independence in Ireland from 1919 to 1921. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-ireland-act-1920">Government of Ireland Act of 1920</a> set up separate home-rule parliaments for Northern and Southern Ireland, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-irish-treaty">Anglo-Irish Treaty</a> of December 1921 provided for an Irish Free State with dominion status. When the Free State came into being on 6 December 1922, the parliament of Northern Ireland exercised its right to opt out the next day, leaving the six north-eastern counties within the United Kingdom. The remaining state was otherwise continuous with its predecessor: the same Crown, Parliament at Westminster, civil service, armed forces and laws. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-and-parliamentary-titles-act-1927">Royal and Parliamentary Titles Act</a> of 1927 changed the formal name to the United <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain</a> and Northern Ireland. In 1922 the state of about 44 million people, under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> and the Conservative Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonar-law">Andrew Bonar Law</a>, still ruled the largest empire in history, held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">mandates</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, Transjordan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, possessed the world's largest navy alongside that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and was a leading member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>, though it was heavily indebted after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/london"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london.jpg" alt="London" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:London_Skyline_(125508655).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ilya Grigorik, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Between the wars the old staple industries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, shipbuilding and iron declined, leaving mass unemployment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-england">North of England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clydeside">Clydeside</a> and Northern Ireland; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/general-strike-of-1926">General Strike</a> of May 1926 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrow-march">Jarrow March</a> of 1936 became symbols of the era. Newer industries such as cars, electrical goods and chemicals grew in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-east-england">South East</a>, and suburban home ownership spread. Women gained the vote on equal terms with men in 1928. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> brought total mobilisation, rationing and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beveridge-report">Beveridge Report</a> of 1942, which inspired the post-war welfare state: the Labour government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-attlee">Clement Attlee</a> nationalised coal, railways and other industries and founded the National Health Service in 1948. Immigration from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, symbolised by the arrival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hmt-empire-windrush">Empire Windrush</a> in 1948, and later from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> and elsewhere, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> an increasingly multi-ethnic society. The affluence of the 1950s and 1960s was followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> and industrial conflict in the 1970s. From 1979 the governments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-thatcher">Margaret Thatcher</a> privatised state industries, curbed trade unions and deregulated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">City of London</a>, accelerating a shift from manufacturing to services and finance. Religious practice declined steadily, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> remains established, and today about 68 million people live in the UK.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-britain"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-britain.jpg" alt="Battle of Britain" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-britain">Battle of Britain</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spitfire_and_He_111_during_Battle_of_Britain_1940.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Australian armed forces, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1922 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> accepted parity with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> under the Washington Naval Treaty, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a>, created in 1918, policed parts of the empire from the air. Rearmament from the mid-1930s produced the Hurricane and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/supermarine-spitfire">Spitfire</a> fighters and the Chain Home radar network. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the United Kingdom declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on 3 September 1939, evacuated over 330,000 Allied troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkirk">Dunkirk</a> in 1940, held off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-britain">Battle of Britain</a> and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a>, fought on until joined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the United States in 1941. British and Commonwealth forces won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-alamein">El Alamein</a> in 1942, fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, and landed in Normandy on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normandy-landings">D-Day</a>, 6 June 1944. About 450,000 British servicemen and civilians died. After 1945 the UK was a founding member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>, tested its first atomic bomb in 1952 and has kept a submarine-based nuclear deterrent since the late 1960s. Its forces fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, in colonial conflicts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>, Kenya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez</a> in 1956 and in the Falklands War of 1982, and were deployed in Northern Ireland from 1969 to 2007. Later operations included the Gulf War of 1991, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> from 2001 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> from 2003.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/human-rights-act-1998"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/human-rights-act-1998.jpg" alt="Human Rights Act 1998" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/human-rights-act-1998">Human Rights Act 1998</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_coat_of_arms_of_the_United_Kingdom_(1952%E2%80%932022,_variant_1).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sodacan, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The United Kingdom is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> without a single written constitution; its constitution rests on statute, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common law</a> and convention. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">Sovereignty</a> lies with the Crown in Parliament, and executive power is exercised by a Prime Minister and Cabinet answerable to the elected House of Commons, chosen by first-past-the-post voting. The power of the unelected House of Lords was limited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-acts-1911-and-1949">Parliament Acts</a> of 1911 and 1949, and most hereditary peers were removed in 1999. The monarchs of the period have been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-viii">Edward VIII</a>, who abdicated in 1936, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-vi">George VI</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a>, the longest-reigning British monarch from 1952 to 2022, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-prince-of-monaco">Charles III</a>. Three legal systems coexist: English law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, Scots law and Northern Irish law. Northern Ireland had its own parliament at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stormont">Stormont</a> until 1972, when it was suspended amid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-troubles">Troubles</a> and direct rule from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> was imposed. Devolution in 1999 created a Scottish Parliament, a National Assembly for Wales and, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/good-friday-agreement">Good Friday Agreement</a> of 1998, a power-sharing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-ireland-assembly">Northern Ireland Assembly</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-rights-act-1998">Human Rights Act</a> of 1998 and the creation of a Supreme Court in 2009 added new judicial checks. A large, permanent, politically neutral civil service, shaped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northcote-trevelyan-report">Northcote–Trevelyan reforms</a> of the nineteenth century, administers government.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bletchley-park"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bletchley-park.jpg" alt="Bletchley Park" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bletchley-park">Bletchley Park</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bletchley_Park_Mansion.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DeFacto, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The United Kingdom's influence in the twentieth century was immense. Its resistance to Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1940–1941, when it was the only major power still fighting, was decisive for the outcome of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and the codebreakers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bletchley-park">Bletchley Park</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alan-turing">Alan Turing</a>, contributed to Allied victory and to the birth of computing. As a permanent member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a>, a founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and a nuclear power, it shaped the post-war order, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-maynard-keynes">John Maynard Keynes</a> helped design the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bretton-woods-system">Bretton Woods system</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">Decolonisation</a> turned most of the empire into independent states, many of which joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-nations">Commonwealth of Nations</a> and kept parliamentary institutions on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-system">Westminster model</a>, English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common law</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-language">English language</a>. British science produced penicillin, the jet engine, the structure of DNA, discovered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> in 1953, and the first cloned mammal, Dolly the sheep. British popular culture, from the BBC, founded in 1922, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-beatles">Beatles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-bond">James Bond</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premier-league">Premier League</a>, reached a global audience, and English became the world's chief language of international communication, a position reinforced by both British and American influence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> remained one of the world's leading financial centres.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-world-war.jpg" alt="Second World War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-646-5188-17,_Flugzeuge_Junkers_Ju_87.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Opitz, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The United Kingdom still exists, but its place in the world has been transformed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> left it victorious but nearly bankrupt, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s independence in 1947 began a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a> that accelerated after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a> of 1956 exposed the limits of British power; most African and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> colonies became independent in the 1960s, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handover-of-hong-kong">handover of Hong Kong</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1997 ended the empire as a major territorial power. Economic growth lagged behind that of Western European rivals, the pound was devalued in 1949 and 1967, and the government sought help from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">International Monetary Fund</a> in 1976. Having declined to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a> at its foundation, the UK entered it in 1973 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-heath">Edward Heath</a> and confirmed membership by referendum in 1975. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/good-friday-agreement">Good Friday Agreement</a> of 1998 largely ended a conflict in Northern Ireland that had cost about 3,500 lives, and Scots voted to remain in the union in 2014. In a referendum in 2016, 51.9 per cent voted to leave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>, and the UK left on 31 January 2020. Today it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-prince-of-monaco">Charles III</a>, one of the world's largest economies, a nuclear power and a permanent member of the Security Council.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The United Kingdom, in its post-1922 form, led the last phase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>, stood against Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1940, founded a welfare state and shaped the post-war international order; its passage from imperial power to a European and then post-European middle power is a central story of twentieth-century history.</em></p>

